From 4dbe1d113ecefcf631a67a4213db9fa82d2f53d6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Valery Petrov <valerypetrov@icloud.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 08:55:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Refuse a distributed remote write on the shard whose target
 is no longer a TimeSeries table

The shard-target check ran on the initiator before the INSERT, and nothing
checked the table each shard resolved when it wrote: a same-schema table
swapped in under a shard-local name between the two took the batch, and
the write was acknowledged into it.

A new query setting, insert_expected_table_engine, moves the engine check
into the delivery path. An INSERT is refused unless the table it names has
that engine, checked after the INSERT grant on the table the INSERT
resolves; once the table passes, the requirement is consumed, so the writes
that table makes on its own (a TimeSeries table's inner tables,
materialized views) are not checked. A Distributed table is not checked
itself but forwards the setting to its shards as any query setting: over
the connection, and on the context copy the sink writes a shard that is
this server itself with. Remote write over a Distributed table sets it to
TimeSeries, so a table swapped in after the initiator's check is refused by
the insert that resolves it, before any row lands, with a retryable 500
that has Prometheus resend the batch. The background sink's local write now
takes a context copy, so the consumption cannot reach the queue headers
written after it.

This replaces the post-delivery re-probe of the previous commit, which only
withheld the acknowledgement after the rows had landed and could not see a
table swapped in and back within one request. A shard-local TimeSeries
table of another time_series type swapped in that late is still not
refused by the shard.

Tests: a stateless test of the setting (the named table, a view's target
through a Distributed table, a local shard in-process and over the
connection), and the two integration tests now assert that nothing lands
in the swapped-in table and that the response carries
UNEXPECTED_TABLE_ENGINE.

